{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchased or previously owned robots becoming weaker after game changes, which fits Power Creep. It also describes matchmaking placing the player against opponents who are far more powerful, indicating manipulated or hidden matchmaking and fitting Illusion of Control; the presence of real-player opponents establishes Competition. The statement about spending thousands to max out options describes a monetary route to gain in-game advantage, supporting Pay to Progress. No labels for Grinding, FOMO, or other mechanics are supported by the text.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "after each change, my robots get weaker and weaker",
      "rationale": "Previously owned robots lose value after game changes."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "unless you're willing to dump thousands into it and max out every option",
      "rationale": "Spending substantial money is described as necessary to reach maximum in-game capability."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the algorithm will put you against real players that are way over powered for you to fight",
      "rationale": "The game makes the player compete against other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the algorithm will put you against real players that are way over powered for you to fight",
      "rationale": "The review attributes outcomes and difficulty to algorithmically manipulated matchmaking."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes players buying their way through the game and obtaining better bots or weapons, which is a payment-based route to progress or advantage, so M_PayToProgress applies. The mention of hacking describes player conduct rather than a game mechanic and is rejected under R2. “Grinding” is a general evaluation without a named repetitive task linked to progress, so T_Grinding is rejected under its boundary rule. Having an inventory full of useless gear does not show value being stranded through capacity limits or a specific shortfall, so M_WasteAversion is rejected.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players who either buy their way through the game",
      "rationale": "Explicitly describes spending money to advance through the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes spending money to obtain a new robot and an advantage over non-spending players, which satisfies Pay to Progress through its Pay to Win indicator. It also describes an acquired robot being nerfed after a month while a new robot is introduced, satisfying Power Creep. The mention of clans and other players defeating the reviewer describes player-versus-player competition, so Competition applies. I rejected Intermediate Currency because no real-money conversion into in-game currency is described, and rejected Grinding, FOMO, and other labels because no qualifying mechanics are stated.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "They will introduce a new robot and you will spend money to get it.",
      "rationale": "Spending money to obtain a new robot is a monetized route to an in-game advantage, matching the Pay to Win indicator."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "one month later, the robot you have get nerfed and another new robot introduced",
      "rationale": "The owned robot loses value through nerfing after a newer robot is introduced."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "clans made out of players who have spent a lot of money will get you",
      "rationale": "The span describes players and clans competing against and defeating the reviewer."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes accumulated bag contents and coins being lost after leaving and returning to the game. This supports T_MandatoryMarathon because stopping the session results in forfeiting held progress, and P_EasyToGetHardToLose because leaving carries the cost of losing accumulated value. M_WasteAversion was considered but rejected under its boundary rule: the review does not describe a finite capacity forcing destruction, an unusable currency remainder, or a purchase shortfall. No bug or technical-fault attribution is present, so R11 does not suppress the labels.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_MandatoryMarathon",
      "span": "I leave the game with my bag full only to come back with it empty",
      "rationale": "The player loses accumulated holdings when ending and later resuming a session, indicating that stopping forfeits progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "I leave the game with my bag full only to come back with it empty and coins that I earned and purchased used",
      "rationale": "Accumulated items and coins are forfeited after leaving, making disengagement costly."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repetitive investment in progression through \"grinding,\" and states that a new update makes previously achieved progress less valuable by giving new players equivalent hero-equipment progress for free; this supports T_Grinding and M_PowerCreep. It also describes purchasing gold and event passes, but does not clearly state that payment is required to progress, that gold is bought with real money as an intermediate currency, or that a recurring pass is being maximized for value, so M_PayToProgress, M_IntermediateCurrency, and M_RecurringFee are rejected under R2 and R4. Playing every day is not described as a daily reward or schedule mechanic, so no temporal appointment label applies.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "After a year of grinding",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes prolonged grinding as the activity invested in progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "Supercell just released an update that invalidates most of the progress I achieved. New players get my current level of hero equipment progress for free, and can easily overtake me",
      "rationale": "The update and free access for new players devalue progress the reviewer had already acquired."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes real money being exchanged for in-game coins, so M_IntermediateCurrency applies. It also describes losing those coins in one round because the dealt cards were not needed, but this does not clearly establish a chance-based monetary wager or a reward-delivery mechanic; therefore M_Gambling, P_RewardMania, and M_WasteAversion are rejected under R2 and their boundary rules. The complaints about poor prize value and high prices alone do not trigger additional labels.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "20$ for only 100k or so coins",
      "rationale": "The span describes paying real money to acquire in-game coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progression blocked by a coin requirement and identifies buying coins as a route to continue, so M_PayToProgress applies. “buying coins” describes real money being converted into in-game currency, so M_IntermediateCurrency also applies. The request to add video earnings or daily rewards is hypothetical and not codable under R12. No repeated activity, daily-return mechanic, reward delivery pattern, or realized leftover currency is described, so the temporal, psychological, and Waste Aversion labels are rejected.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "I can't seem to get past it without using coins, which I have run out of, or buying coins",
      "rationale": "The review describes progression being blocked unless the player spends coins or buys more."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "buying coins",
      "rationale": "The span describes acquiring in-game coins through a purchase with real money."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes requesting a resource from a clan, which satisfies Reciprocity. It also describes misleading promotional wording that makes the player expect a larger coin reward, which satisfies Aesthetic Manipulation because the deception is in the wording. I considered UI Misdirection for the control changing to a higher value, but the review does not clearly connect that interface behavior to a purchase or monetary opt-out, so it is rejected under the label boundary rule. Intermediate Currency, Gambling, and Easy to Purchase are not assigned because no real-money conversion, wager, or purchase-friction mechanism is clearly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "easier to ask our clan for a stamp",
      "rationale": "The player describes asking clan members for an in-game resource, which is a resource-transfer or favor-exchange mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_AestheticManipulation",
      "span": "The wording makes you think you are going to get more than you think.",
      "rationale": "The promotional wording is described as misleading the player about the reward they would receive."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes matchmaking that allegedly assigns teammates according to a hidden and illogical rule, which fits Illusion of Control because the game presents this as skill-based matching while manipulating matchmaking outcomes. Matchmaking also describes multiplayer competition, so Competition applies. I rejected Pay to Progress because 'they only need money' does not describe payment being required for progress, and rejected Grinding because 'waste of time and energy' is a general evaluation without a repetitive progress activity.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "The match making, completely illogical,if you play good,game will give you bad teammates expecting you to carry and if you play bad,game will give you more bad teammates saying that it's so called \"skill based matching\"",
      "rationale": "The span describes matchmaking being tuned by a hidden or misleading rule, making the purported skill-based outcome deceptive."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "The match making",
      "rationale": "Matchmaking with teammates describes the game's player-versus-player competitive structure."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an additional in-game download after the reviewer believed the game file was already downloaded, which matches Fragmented Downloads. The matchmaking problem is attributed to a technical/network issue and is not coded under R11. No other mechanic labels are supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "I thought I already download the file but I still get an additional download",
      "rationale": "Describes a mandatory additional in-game download after the initial download, obscuring the game's true download requirements."
    }
  ],
