  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a friend-invitation mechanic tied to rewards, which satisfies Forced Fellowship. It also describes wagering dice through “played max bet” with luck-based outcomes, supporting Gambling. Repeated duplicate stickers describe unpredictable or repeated reward delivery, supporting Reward Mania. The login failure is attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a malfunction and does not receive a label under R11. No Daily Rewards, Pay to Progress, Competition, or Friend Spam / Impersonation mechanic is clearly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rationale": "The game rewards players for recruiting friends to join, matching the Social Pyramid Scheme indicator."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK!",
      "rationale": "The span describes staking dice in a maximum bet with luck determining the outcome."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rationale": "Repeated duplicate sticker rewards describe a variable or repeated reward-delivery mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-day expiration schedule that causes collected cards to disappear and requires recollection, so T_PlayingByAppointment applies because the game's clock imposes a deadline and loss. It also describes a compulsion to obtain the full card set, supporting P_CompleteTheCollection. M_DeceptiveLuxury is rejected because the rarity and scarcity are not tied to price or purchase. S_Reciprocity is rejected because trading is mentioned without an obligation to reciprocate. P_EasyToGetHardToLose is rejected because the reviewer is uninstalling as the collection is about to be lost, rather than describing reluctance to leave due to accumulated investment. P_RewardMania is rejected because no random delivery or repeated-attempt reward mechanism is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "all the cards you collect disappear after 30 days. So you have to collect them all over again",
      "rationale": "A game-imposed 30-day expiry causes collected rewards to be lost and requires timely completion or recollection."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you lose all you have before getting the 162",
      "rationale": "The span describes pursuing the complete set of 162 cards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes upgrade timers that require waiting and payment to run two building upgrades simultaneously. This supports T_PlayingByAppointment because the game imposes a wait through timers, and M_PayToProgress because paying is presented as the route to advance an additional building upgrade at once. The thumbnail and trailer mismatch is out-of-game marketing, so it is excluded under R13. 'Same old stuff' and 'time waster' are evaluations without a specific repetitive progress activity, so T_Grinding is rejected under R2.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "Hit upgrade wait",
      "rationale": "The span describes an in-game upgrade timer that imposes a wait before progress continues."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "want to do 2 buildings at once then pay $",
      "rationale": "The span describes paying to perform an additional building upgrade simultaneously, accelerating progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes accumulated time and money making quitting feel too costly, which satisfies P_EasyToGetHardToLose. The statements that the game is a scam, that it is addictive, and that the player keeps playing are evaluative or general psychological claims without a specific codable mechanic. No temporal, monetary, social, or technical mechanic is otherwise described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "by the time you want to quit, you would have invested too much time and money into this game to simply quit. You would feel like you're wasting your money if you just quit!",
      "rationale": "The span describes accumulated time and money raising the cost of leaving the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes paid shields and paid upgrades as necessary for protection and competitive advantage, so M_PayToProgress applies. It also describes higher-level players attacking the reviewer, which establishes player-versus-player competition, so S_Competition applies. M_IntermediateCurrency is rejected because the review mentions gems but does not describe real money being converted into gems. M_RecurringFee is rejected because temporary shield duration is not a subscription or battle pass. S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior is rejected because harmful or unfair attacks are described, but no reward or incentive for the aggressor is stated. T_PlayingByAppointment is rejected because shield duration alone does not require the player to play at specific times.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you're not willing to pay for upgrades, then this game's not for you.",
      "rationale": "The span describes payment as necessary to continue progressing or competing effectively."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "Players with higher levels will turn you into a punching bag.",
      "rationale": "The span describes players competing against and attacking one another."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertisements as a condition for claiming a daily reward, which satisfies T_Advertisement because the player is compelled to watch an ad for a reward. It also describes a daily reward mechanic, satisfying T_DailyRewards. The phrase \"paid popups\" is only a general purchase solicitation and does not establish an Easy to Purchase mechanism under its boundary rule.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "watch an ad just to claim a daily reward",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to watch an advertisement to obtain an in-game reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "a daily reward",
      "rationale": "The game provides a reward associated with daily play or claiming."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchase offers that appear before gameplay, payment as necessary for progression, rewarded ads as the route to free-to-play benefits, and a shifting meta requiring the newest paid bot. I considered M_EasyToPurchase because the offers intercept play, M_PayToProgress because progression is tied to payment, T_Advertisement because ads are described as providing rewards, and M_PowerCreep because newer bots displace older ones in the meta. I rejected S_Competition because no competition mechanic is described, and S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO because falling behind is mentioned as a consequence of the meta rather than anticipatory pressure to return or continue playing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game",
      "rationale": "Purchase offers intercept the player before gameplay, reducing the friction of being prompted to buy."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck!",
      "rationale": "The span describes progression as requiring payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it",
      "rationale": "The shifting meta and need for the newest bot indicate that newer releases reduce the value or viability of existing bots, driving purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rationale": "The span describes ads as the mechanism through which the player receives free-to-play rewards, indicating incentivized ad viewing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes payment providing competitive advantage, so M_PayToProgress applies. \"update times for your base\" describes in-game base timers, supporting T_PlayingByAppointment. \"Everything scales endlessly\" and having no endpoint beyond more progression support T_InfiniteTreadmill, but \"more grind\" alone does not identify a repetitive activity, so T_Grinding is rejected under its boundary rule. The deliberately confusing UI tied to buying items supports M_UIMisdirection. Alliances and attacks describe player-versus-player conflict, so S_Competition applies. Forced Fellowship and S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior are rejected: the review does not describe recruitment or obligation to avoid letting teammates down, nor a reward flowing to aggressors for bullying.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rationale": "The span describes spending money as a route to an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rationale": "The span describes base progression being governed by in-game timers that impose waiting."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rationale": "The span describes progression that continually expands without an attainable endpoint."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes confusing interface design directing players toward purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rationale": "The span describes players or alliances being placed in hostile competition with one another."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a large monetary requirement to play and accumulated time investment making players reluctant to leave. I considered M_PayToProgress for \"Unless you have 50-100k to throw into the game,\" but the span does not explicitly tie payment to progress, continued play, or overcoming an in-game obstacle, so R2 and the Pay to Progress definition do not support assigning it. The final statement directly describes investment making departure difficult, satisfying P_EasyToGetHardToLose. The claimed stealing of earned items is not assigned because it describes loss, not a mechanic that raises the cost of leaving. No other candidate label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "most are too invested to leave",
      "rationale": "The reviewer says accumulated investment makes players reluctant or unable to abandon the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
